  "snippet": "#​626 — November 27, 2026 Read the Web Version Go Weekly An Overview of Go's Tooling, Updated for 2026 — Alex's 2019 tour of Go tooling was one of our most popular links ever, but now he's back with an all-new 2026 editi...",
  "coverImage": "https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/y6z6dqzbfy2uzig73q9i.jpg",
  "readTime": 1,
  "content": "<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" style=\"padding-left: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p>#​626 — November 27, 2026</p></td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"padding-right: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193561/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">Read the Web Version</a></p></td>\n</tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0 12px;\"><p>Go Weekly</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193520/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/y6z6dqzbfy2uzig73q9i.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193520/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.1em; line-height: 1.4em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.alexedwards.net\">An Overview of Go's Tooling, Updated for 2026</a></span> — Alex's <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193521/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">2019 tour of Go tooling</a> was one of our most popular links ever, but now he's back with an all-new 2026 edition. He covers numerous areas in detail from testing and code quality to benchmarking, refactoring, integrating with AI coding assistants, and more. An epic post!</p>\n<p>Alex Edwards </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193519/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\"><img height=\"110\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/c_limit,w_480,h_480,q_auto/copm/bb8b81f9.png\" style=\"padding-top: 12px; padding-left: 12px; line-height: 100%;\" width=\"110\"/></a>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193519/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"master.dev\">Design Backend Systems That Scale</a></span> — Every architecture decision is a tradeoff. Jem Young (Netflix) gives you a repeatable framework for scoping requirements, scaling data, and diagramming distributed systems, whether you’re on the job or in a system design interview.</p>\n<p>master.dev <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #885 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193522/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"traefik.io\">Yaegi v2: <em>'The Most Complete Go Interpreter'</em></a></span> — Traefik's embeddable Go interpreter has been rewritten from scratch and in <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193562/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">a simpler, more logical way</a> with <code>go/types</code> and Go's SSA package doing the heavy lifting. It also passes 1044 of 1066 programs in Go's test suite (up from 641 previously).</p>\n<p>Salleyron and Vauge (Traefik Labs) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>IN BRIEF:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Go 1.28's scheduled <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193523/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">development freeze has begun</a>, with RC1 'tentatively scheduled' for the week of December 10.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Following last week's <em>'likely accept'</em>, the <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193524/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">stable SIMD API is now enabled by default</a> for Go 1.28, with <code>GOEXPERIMENT=nosimdstable</code> for turning it off.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fixing a seven year old complaint, Go 1.28's <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193525/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">GOCACHE build cache will trim itself as it grows</a> to help keep the size in check.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>JetBrains has opened up <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193527/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">GoLand's code intelligence to VS Code and Cursor users</a> in a free public beta preview.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193528/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"internals-for-interns.com\">How <code>encoding/json/v2</code> Works Internally</a></span> — The Go team's <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193529/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">explained how the new JSON API works</a>, but Jesús goes a bit deeper and follows a JSON document through the new stack, pointing out what's going on along the way.</p>\n<p>Jesús Espino </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193530/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"link.svix.com\">Why Every Frontier Lab Uses Our Webhook Standard</a></span> — OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and xAI all sign webhooks the same way. See what each ships and why none rolled their own.</p>\n<p>Svix <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #885 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193531/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Probabilistic Data Structures in Go – Part 2</a> – A follow-up to our featured item last week that moves on to binary fuse filters, UltraLogLog, MinHash and more. <cite>Anton Zhiyanov</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193532/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">The Performance Penalty in Go's Default HTTP Client</a> – Why two idle connections per host can have the kernel looking for free ports. <cite>Mansoor</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193533/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">HTTP/3 From Scratch, Part 2</a> – Getting a browser to actually use HTTP/3, then building a full server and client in Go. <cite>Kevin McDonald</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193534/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Letting Packagers Override Your Go Program's Version</a> <cite>Jamie Tanna</cite></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0;\"><p>🛠 Code &amp; Tools</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193535/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/urhrtstiz5vzfvcjs2fc.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193535/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"jespino.github.io\">gopgext: Write Postgres Extensions in Go</a></span> — It's amazing what you can do with <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193536/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">TinyGo</a> and Jesús Espino has been experimenting a lot recently! <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193535/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">gopgext</a> uses a custom TinyGo target to turn plain Go (with marked functions becoming SQL functions) into Postgres extensions.</p>\n<p>Jesús Espino </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 <strong>And a way to write Linux kernel modules in Go too.</strong> Jesús's other experiment, <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193537/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">gokm</a>, takes a similar approach for building Linux kernel modules as a way to create devices, filesystems, network interfaces and more.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193538/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">Ekuma: A Pure Go JavaScript Engine</a></span> — From Ebitengine's creator comes a new, cgo-free JavaScript engine covering nearly all of ES2026 (the latest JavaScript language standard). It passes 99.99% of the test262 suite, and benchmarks ~8x faster than the more established <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193539/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">goja</a>.</p>\n<p>Hajime Hoshi </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193540/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">errortype: Find Inconsistent Uses of Error Types</a></span> — A linter that checks error types are used consistently as either a value or a pointer, as a mismatch can trip up <code>errors.As</code>. <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193541/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">v0.0.12</a> can rename errors to Go's <code>ErrX</code>/<code>XError</code> conventions, and flags code that <code>go vet</code> will report if your <code>go.mod</code> uses 1.27+.</p>\n<p>Fillmore Labs </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.2em !important; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193542/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"fzakaria.com\">tabc: Running Containers in Browser Tabs</a></span> — A Go-built OCI runtime that runs containers on a WASM-compiled Linux kernel inside a headless Chromium instance making it possible to run images on a Mac, say, without needing a typical Linux VM. <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193543/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4;\" target=\"_blank\">GitHub repo</a>.</p>\n<p>Farid Zakaria </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193544/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Bubble Tea 2.2</a> – The popular TUI framework can now tell the terminal how to resume your program after a restart (via <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193545/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">the OSC 7577 standard</a>, which <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193546/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Rex</a> and <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193563/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Herdr</a> support).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193547/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">go-redis 9.24</a> – The official Redis client library gains Redis 8.12 commands and an experimental Active-Active failover client.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193548/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">gotreesitter 0.58</a> – Pure Go tree-sitter runtime whose output now matches C tree-sitter's for 34 grammars.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193549/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">quic-go 0.66</a> – The pure Go QUIC and HTTP/3 implementation lands a large batch of RFC compliance fixes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193550/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">oapi-codegen 2.9</a> – Tool to generate Go clients/servers from OpenAPI specs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193551/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Expr 1.18</a> – Expression language and evaluation engine for Go.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px;\">\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📰 Classifieds</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193552/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">CodeAF</a> is one native Go binary that runs every coding task in its own isolated copy of the repo. #1 of ten on DeepSWE, lowest cost per fix.</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0;\"><p>📢  Elsewhere in the ecosystem</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The Next.js team has <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193553/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">merged an experimental Go-powered server</a>, now <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193554/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">called Switchyard</a>, that sits in front of <code>next start</code>. It only proxies back to Node.js for now, but the plan is to move routing into Go.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193555/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">minipg</a> offers an interesting approach to using Postgres as a single executable, with a <code>.pgdb</code> directory holding the database. Could be a handy way to spin up Postgres temporarily in development and testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If your Go tests use Docker's <code>postgres:latest</code> image, note <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193556/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">it now points at Postgres 19</a>, so a new container using that image with a not yet upgraded v18 data volume will fail to start.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>And while I'm on a run of Postgres updates, if you use Postgres at all, be sure to check out our sister newsletter <a href=\"https://golangweekly.com/link/193557/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0099b4; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Postgres Weekly</a>!</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n</td></tr>\n</table>\n",
  "attachments": []
}